<project name="tbellini-m">
  TBellini is a private, invite-only site for one friend group. Members record the events they
  and others are going to, and build a shared archive around them: who was there, where it
  happened, the music, and the memories attached to every event, place, and person.
  Most events are music festivals and shows, so music is a first-class part of the archive.

  The site is one shared space. Anyone with an account sees essentially everything; there is
  no per-user private data, no public access, and no public feed. A user account exists mainly
  so a person can log in (plus a display name so others know who they are).

  How to maintain this file:
  - Keep sections in this order; add new entities, views, or services as another
    detail inside the matching section.
  - Mark items with a status: [live] implemented in code, [partial] in code but incomplete or
    broken, [planned] agreed roadmap, [proposed] an idea not yet decided,
    [remove] still in code but no longer wanted (see the next-steps plan).
  - The "Next steps plan" near the end is the working plan; update the phase status as work lands.
    The living step log is plans/STATUS.md (plan: plans/mvp-go-live.md).
  - Deep detail lives in docs/; this file is the overview. Last reviewed: 2026-11-14 on branch
    feature/base-tbellini-site at commit b0de4bd (phases 0 and 1a-1e done; Phase 2 code-ready, nothing provisioned).

  <goal>
    - Let the group see, in one place, which events are coming up and who is Going or Interested.
    - Turn past events into a lasting shared archive: places, artists and sets, photos, and memories,
      all linked so any node (event, member, place, artist) shows its history.
    - Make the music side central: what artists were seen, at which festival or show,
      with whom, and the songs or sets that defined the moment.
    - Keep it exclusive: only invited people can create an account; nothing is visible without signing in.
  </goal>

  <context>
    - Status: the M0 skeleton (events, calendar, RSVPs, invite-only signup) plus the shared-space model
      (phase 1: database, code, UI, graph projector, docs) is built and tested locally and in demo mode;
      a local-stack browser e2e passed on 2026-11-14. Phase 2 (go live) is code-ready but NOT provisioned:
      no hosted Supabase, Vercel, Cloudflare or Neo4j, no accounts, and no production data. Phase 2 was
      replanned on 2026-11-14 (plans/mvp-go-live.md): scripted, env-var-driven provisioning; every provisioning
      run needs the user's approval first (see plans/STATUS.md).
    - The old community, membership, and private-people model is gone from the database (migration
      20261017000000_shared_space.sql), code, UI, graph projector, and docs.
    - Two run modes, chosen by NEXT_PUBLIC_ARCHIVE_MODE:
      demo (browser localStorage, simulated members Sam and Alex) and supabase (real auth/data).
    - Linear work stays inside the tbellini-m Linear project (capability parents labeled Feature,
      User story children).
    - Original static-site files index.html and web.css are historical; the app lives in src/app.
    <detail name="Key documents">
      PROJECT_MEMORY.md (product decisions, moved to the shared-space model), plans/mvp-go-live.md (stable plan)
      and plans/STATUS.md (living step log), docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/RUNBOOK.md, docs/ROADMAP.md, docs/PROJECT_HANDOFF.md,
      docs/VALIDATION.md, docs/CONTRIBUTING.md, docs/conversations/ (original planning transcript).
    </detail>
  </context>

  <stack>
    - TypeScript 5.9, Next.js 16 (App Router), React 19, Node.js >=22.18 (package.json engines), npm.
    - just (justfile) is the task runner: setup, dev, checks, database and hosted-ops recipes.
    - Zod 4 for validation at every boundary (forms, returned DB rows, snapshots).
    - Supabase JS 2.117 / SSR 0.12 for auth and data; neo4j-driver 6.2 for the graph runner.
    - Vitest 4 plus node:test for scripts and worker; ESLint 9 with eslint-config-next.
    - Plain CSS in src/app/globals.css with a warm palette (pink, lavender, mango, ink); serif
      headings. Preserve this look.
  </stack>

  <integrations>
    Every service below is configured in the repo, but none is provisioned or deployed yet.

    <detail name="Vercel — app hosting">
      Hosts the Next.js app through Vercel's Git integration (preview deploys per branch, production from
      the main branch). Next.js preset, Node 22 (>=22.18 per engines), npm run build. Set the three NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars per
      environment; they are baked in at build time, so rebuild after changes. App rollback means
      redeploying the previous Vercel deployment. There is no separate deploy workflow.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Supabase — database, auth (source of truth)">
      Postgres holds all authoritative data; Supabase Auth provides email/password accounts.
      Row-level security (RLS) allows only signed-in members (a user with a profile) to read data,
      and limits which rows each user may change (for example, only your own RSVP).
      The browser uses the publishable key plus the user's session; the service-role key is used
      only by the graph runner and admin tasks. Migrations live in supabase/migrations and only move
      forward; SQL tests live in supabase/tests. The CLI is pinned at 2.119.0.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Neo4j — connection graph (derived)">
      A rebuildable projection of entities and relationships, used for "how is this connected"
      features (M3). Postgres triggers write changes to graph_outbox in the same transaction.
      scripts/sync-graph.mjs replays them in order under a database mutex, using idempotent Cypher.
      The app never depends on Neo4j being up, and the browser never queries it directly.
      Neo4j Aura with TLS is the expected host. Not provisioned: the shared-space projector is built and
      unit-tested (node tests) but has not run against a real Neo4j; a graph built by the old projector
      needs a wipe and full replay because node keys changed.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Cloudflare — DNS, optional edge worker, future media">
      DNS for the custom domain, pointing at Vercel (start with DNS-only records).
      The worker tbellini-m-edge (cloudflare/) currently serves only GET /health.
      Planned: R2 object storage for photos and video via server-issued upload URLs (M2), and
      possibly Queues. The MediaStorage port exists; no binding exists yet.
    </detail>
    <detail name="GitHub Actions — CI and manual ops">
      ci.yml runs on pull requests and on pushes to main, with an app job (lint, typecheck, test, build)
      and a database job (local Supabase reset plus SQL tests). The main branch here is
      site-code, which the push filter now targets. graph-sync.yml and cloudflare.yml run only on manual
      dispatch, are gated by repository variables, and use the protected "production" environment.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Linear — roadmap and issues">
      The tbellini-m project holds the M0–M5 roadmap. Check its current state before relying on the
      historical claims in PROJECT_MEMORY.md, and align it with the next-steps plan below.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Spotify — music data [planned, phase 4]">
      Chosen music provider (decision dated 2026-10-17). Uses the Spotify Web API from the server only (a Next.js route
      or worker) with app client credentials, for artist and track search and metadata (name, image,
      genres). Stores the Spotify ID on Artist and Track records; our own UUIDs stay the primary keys.
      Embeds Spotify players on artist, event, and memory pages. Possible later: an event playlist and member Spotify
      login. Secrets: SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID and SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET (server-only, never NEXT_PUBLIC_).
      Setlists are not in Spotify; they are entered manually unless setlist.fm is added later.
    </detail>
  </integrations>

  <architecture>
    Layers (the dependency arrows point inward):
    - Domain (src/domain): entities, Zod schemas, repository interfaces, ArchiveService
      use cases, time handling. No SDK imports.
    - Infrastructure (src/infrastructure): demo (localStorage) and Supabase adapters that
      implement the same repository contracts; read-all pagination (1,000-row pages).
    - Presentation (src/components, src/app): ArchiveApp chooses the provider and handles auth,
      snapshot state, and guards against stale requests; view components only render and call callbacks.
    - Ops scripts (scripts/): the graph outbox replay and Cypher projection, and seed-invite.mjs (creates the
      first invitation), run with service credentials.
    - Edge (cloudflare/): a reserved worker boundary.

    Data flow: browser → ArchiveService (validate + permission check) → repository adapter →
    Supabase (RLS re-checks) → triggers → graph_outbox → manual sync → Neo4j.
    The app loads a whole snapshot (events, members, people, tags, RSVPs) and reloads it after each change.
    There is no realtime yet.

    <detail name="Extension pattern">
      Each new capability adds: a domain contract and schema, an ArchiveService use case, a demo
      adapter and a Supabase adapter, a forward migration with RLS and SQL tests, outbox types with
      matching projector code and tests, and a UI view. Do not grow the event repository into
      the whole archive; add cohesive modules (places, music, memories) that reference IDs.
    </detail>
  </architecture>

  <data name="Entities — target model">
    All records use UUID IDs and timestamps. Content records carry createdBy (the member who added
    it) and provenance ('manual'). Times are ISO instants stored as timestamptz, plus the original
    IANA timezone used for display and calendar grouping. All content is visible to every member.

    <detail name="Member (profiles) [live]">
      The login identity: id = the Supabase auth user id, plus displayName (1–200 characters). Email stays
      in Supabase Auth and is never copied. Having a profile is what makes someone a member; there is no
      separate community or membership record. Each user edits only their own name.
      Every member automatically gets a linked Person (ensure_member_person), so people are one kind of
      node whether or not they have an account.
      Later: optional avatar, birth details (for astrology), and favorite artists.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Invitation (invitations) [live]">
      Fields: code (8–12 uppercase letters, digits, dashes), created_by, expires_at, used_at, used_by, person_id
      (optional: the invite names an existing Person to claim). Any member creates one from the Members view
      through the create_invitation(expires_in_days, for_person) database function
      (randomness from gen_random_uuid, single use, 1–30 days, default 7). Signup requires an unused,
      unexpired code (entry is case-insensitive); the signup trigger (join_tbellini) consumes it atomically,
      records who used it, then creates the profile and its Person (or links the named Person).
      RLS is on, and clients have no table access.
      Demo mode shows random codes (crypto.getRandomValues) for display only; it has no signup flow.
      The first account's invite is seeded with `just seed-invite confirm=hosted` (scripts/seed-invite.mjs;
      service-role key; `just seed-invite-local` for the local stack); the script is tested but has not
      been run against a hosted project (docs/RUNBOOK.md, "Invite-only signup and the first account").
    </detail>
    <detail name="Event (events) [live]">
      Fields: title (1–200 characters), description (up to 10k), startsAt, endsAt (optional, at or
      after the start), timezone, location (free text up to 500 characters; becomes a Place in M1).
      Any member can create events. Only the creator can edit or delete one (decided 2026-10-17).
    </detail>
    <detail name="Person (people) [live]">
      A shared node for a real person: displayName, notes, createdBy (nullable if the creator's profile is
      deleted), and memberId (unique; empty until they get an account). Anyone can add a person who doesn't
      have an account (a friend, a partner). Every Member automatically gets a Person at signup
      (ensure_member_person), so tags and attendance always point at Person. Claiming a non-member Person
      works two ways: the invite names the Person (invitations.person_id), or the new member picks "this is me"
      (person_claims) and the Person's creator confirms (confirm_person_claim RPC), which links the Person and
      carries their history over. Only the creator edits a Person (clients may change only displayName and notes);
      memberId is never client-writable. The claim UI (People page, person detail) is built.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Tag (event_person_tags) [live]">
      Links an event to a Person who is part of it, with the field taggedBy, so people without accounts can be
      tagged. Replaces event_people and event_user_tags (data converted, old tables dropped). Only the event's
      creator can tag; a tag does NOT imply an RSVP.
    </detail>
    <detail name="RSVP (event_rsvps) [live]">
      Links an event to a member with a status: going or interested (no row means no response).
      Everyone sees all RSVPs; each user sets or clears only their own. This drives My calendar.
    </detail>
    <detail name="System tables [live]">
      graph_outbox (ordered change and tombstone queue) and graph_sync_lock (singleton replay mutex).
      Service role only.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Person claims (person_claims) [live]">
      PK (person_id, claimant_id), claimant unique. A member requests a claim on an unlinked Person; the claimant
      or the Person's creator may cancel; the creator confirms through confirm_person_claim.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Removed: Community, Membership, private Person and Attendance [done]">
      The shared-space migration dropped communities, memberships, is_community_member, the events.community_id
      and visibility columns, and merged the owner-private people and event_people into the shared Person and Tag.
      The TBELLINI_COMMUNITY_ID constant, CommunityMembership and Attendance are gone from the code. There is one
      group, and nothing is private per user. graph_outbox still permits the retired attendance and user_tag
      types, which the projector skips with a logged reason.
    </detail>
  </data>

  <data name="Relationships">
    Current scope [live]:
    - Member 1—* Event (createdBy)
    - Member 1—1 Person (every Member gets a Person at signup; memberId set when someone signs up or claims an existing Person)
    - Event *—* Person via Tag (taggedBy recorded; works for people without accounts)
    - Event *—* Member via RSVP (status on the link; self-managed; RSVP needs an account)
    - Member 1—* Invitation (created_by); Invitation 0..1—1 Member (used_by, set at signup); Invitation 0..1—1 Person (person_id: the Person it lets the new member claim)
    - Person 0..*—1 claimant Member via person_claims (confirmed by the Person's creator)

    Neo4j projection [live in code and tests, manual sync; not provisioned]:
    - Nodes: ArchiveEntity:Event, ArchiveEntity:Profile, ArchiveEntity:Person (keys event:id, profile:id,
      person:id; Person is shared).
    - Edges: (Person)-[:TAGGED_IN]->(Event), (Profile)-[:RSVP {status}]->(Event),
      (Profile)-[:IS]->(Person). The old private ATTENDED edge is removed (retired outbox types are skipped).
    - Projected fields: IDs, title, display name, updatedAt, RSVP status. Never email.

    Planned [proposed edge names]:
    - Event -[:AT]-> Place; Event -[:EDITION_OF]-> Festival/Series; Event -[:PART_OF]-> Event (festival days)
    - Performance -[:BY]-> Artist; Performance -[:AT]-> Event; Performance -[:PLAYED]-> Track
    - Person -[:SAW]-> Performance; Person -[:WENT_TO]-> Event (confirmed attendance after the fact)
    - Memory -[:ABOUT]-> Event | Person | Place | Artist | Performance; Memory -[:BY]-> Member
    - Media -[:ATTACHED_TO]-> Event | Memory | Place | Performance
    - Collection -[:CONTAINS]-> any node; Label -[:LABELS]-> any node
  </data>

  <data name="Planned entities [planned / proposed]">
    These come from the roadmap (M1–M5) and the music-first direction. Fields are a starting sketch;
    refine them when each phase begins. All are shared with every member.

    <detail name="Place [planned M1]">
      A venue or location (name, kind: venue/festival grounds/city/home, address or geo, timezone).
      Replaces free-text event location; an event can have several places. Each place has a history of
      events held there.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Festival / Series [planned M1; proposed name]">
      A recurring festival or tour across years. Each yearly Event is an edition; a multi-day
      festival can have child events (days, stages).
    </detail>
    <detail name="Attendance — went to [proposed M1]">
      After an event, record who actually went (separate from the before-the-event Going RSVP). Points at
      Person, so people without accounts have a history too. Any member can record that they went
      themselves; the event creator can record it for others.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Artist [planned M2]">
      A musician, band, or DJ: name, spotifyId, genres, image (from Spotify). Has a hub page showing
      every time the group saw them.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Performance / Set [proposed M2]">
      An artist playing at an event: stage, day, set time, optional setlist. The key link between Event
      and Artist; the event's lineup is its list of performances.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Track / Playlist [proposed M2]">
      Songs tied to performances, memories, or events (for example, "the song of the weekend"), stored with
      spotifyId and embeddable; possibly a generated Spotify playlist per event.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Media [planned M2]">
      Photos, video, and external links stored in R2 (via MediaStorage), with uploadedBy and capturedAt.
      Attached to events, memories, places, or performances.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Memory [planned M3]">
      A story, note, or moment written by a member, linked to one or more events, members, places,
      artists, or performances, with optional media. The connected-memory core.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Collection, Label [planned M5]">
      Member-made groupings (for example, "Summer 2025 run") and labels across the whole archive.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Astrology [planned M5]">
      Astrology tied to members and dates (for example, birth charts or the sky at an event). Scope still to be defined.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Archive keeping [planned M5]">
      Approximate dates (a new explicit model, not fake timestamps), provenance beyond 'manual',
      historical import (past shows), incomplete-record cleanup, export.
    </detail>
  </data>

  <access>
    - Only invited people can create an account; anonymous visitors see nothing.
    - Signed in = member = can read everything and add content.
    - Edit and delete: only the person who created a record (decided 2026-10-17 for events and their tags).
      By default the same rule applies to new content types (places, artists, memories, media) unless decided otherwise.
    - RSVP: each member sets or clears only their own status.
    - Profile: each member edits only their own display name.
    - Invitations: any member can create one (decided 2026-10-17). There is no admin role.
  </access>

  <privacy>
    - Everything is shared among members; nothing is public. Treat the whole archive as private to the group.
    - Email addresses stay in Supabase Auth and are not shown in the UI or copied to Neo4j.
    - Neo4j and backups hold group content; restrict their access to the operators.
    - Demo mode is local to one browser and not a security boundary.
    - Never log credentials.
  </privacy>

  <frontend name="Pages and views">
    Single page app shell (src/app/page.tsx → ArchiveApp) with a sidebar nav, a top bar (mode badge,
    sign-out, member switcher in demo), and the main content. Navigation uses view state, not URL routes.

    <detail name="Sign in / Sign up [live]">
      Email and password. Signup asks for a display name and an invite code (stored uppercase). If email
      confirmation is required, the user is told to confirm, then sign in. No OAuth or magic link.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Events (default) [live]">
      A time-ordered list of all events, shown as cards (date tile, title, time, location, RSVP counts).
      Has a "+ Create event" button.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Calendar [live]">
      A month grid of all events, grouped by the date in each event's recorded timezone; click an event to
      open its details.
    </detail>
    <detail name="My calendar [live]">
      The same grid, filtered to events where the current user is Going or Interested. Tags and
      authorship never add events here. The empty state links to Events.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Event detail [live]">
      Title, time, location, description, and creator. Shows your RSVP (Going / Interested / Clear),
      tagged people, and who is Going or Interested. The creator also gets Edit, Delete, and a tag picker
      that can choose any Person or add a new one.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Event form [live]">
      Validated create and edit form with useful field errors; time input in the event's timezone.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Members [live]">
      A member list, editing of your own display name, and "Invite New Members" (generate and copy an invite code,
      optionally naming a Person to claim). Open: the generated code is shown only behind a Copy button, not as text.
    </detail>
    <detail name="People [live]">
      A shared People page: every person, with or without an account, plus create, and edit by the creator.
      Members show an "account" badge. Person detail has the claim flow ("this is me" request, creator confirms).
      It may merge with Members later.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Error boundary and health [live]">
      src/app/error.tsx offers route-level recovery; authenticated load errors offer Retry or Sign out.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Future views [planned / proposed]">
      - Person page (M1): for members and non-members alike; events they went to or are going to, artists seen,
        memories about them.
      - Place page (M1): events held there over time, memories, media.
      - Festival page (M1/M2): editions by year, lineups, who went each year.
      - Artist page (M2): every performance the group saw, who was there, setlists, media.
      - Event lineup and "who saw what" (M2): performances on the event page; mark sets you saw.
      - Media gallery (M2): per event, per artist.
      - Memories (M3): composer that links to any node; a memory list on every node page; "why is this connected".
      - Timeline and search (M4): one timeline of everything, with filters; search across all entities.
      - Collections (M5).
      Add real URL routes (for example, /events/[id], /artists/[id], /places/[id], /members/[id]) before
      the node pages, so links can be shared inside the group.
    </detail>
  </frontend>

  <api>
    - GET /api/health: 200 when the mode and config are valid, 503 otherwise. In supabase mode it also makes a short
      Supabase ping (src/lib/health.ts; unit-tested, not yet run against a live project). Never probes Neo4j.
    - Worker GET /health: checks only that the handler responds.
    - There are no other server endpoints; data access goes from the browser to Supabase under RLS.
    - Future: invite creation via a database function or server route, media upload URL issuance, music
      provider lookups (server-side, to keep API keys secret), and a graph read API for members only.
  </api>

  <development>
    - Preferred: `just` lists recipes. just setup (npm ci, create .env.local); just dev (demo mode,
      http://localhost:3000); just dev-supabase; just check (lint, typecheck, test, build); just ci (check plus db
      reset/test/stop); just db-start / db-stop / db-reset / db-test / db-diff; just health; just clean.
      Hosted recipes need confirm=hosted: just db-push, graph-sync, replay-outbox, seed-invite.
      just seed-invite-local seeds the first invite on the local stack.
    - Without just: npm ci; cp .env.example .env.local; npm run dev → http://localhost:3000 (demo mode).
    - Local Supabase: npx --yes supabase@2.119.0 start / db reset --local / test db / stop
      (Docker required). Then set the mode to supabase plus the local URL and publishable key in .env.local.
    - Graph sync: node --env-file=.env.local scripts/sync-graph.mjs (needs the service role and Neo4j settings).
    - Work in small slices; every change keeps the demo and Supabase adapters behaviorally identical.
    <configuration>
      Public (browser): NEXT_PUBLIC_ARCHIVE_MODE (demo | supabase), NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL,
      NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY. Server/ops only: SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY,
      NEO4J_URI, NEO4J_USERNAME, NEO4J_PASSWORD, NEO4J_DATABASE. GitHub: NEO4J_SYNC_ENABLED,
      CLOUDFLARE_DEPLOY_ENABLED, SUPABASE_URL, CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN, CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID.
      seed-invite also reads NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, plus optional INVITE_CODE and
      INVITE_EXPIRY_DAYS.
      Planned (phase 4): SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID, SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET (server-only).
      Never commit real values; .env.example holds placeholders only.
    </configuration>
  </development>

  <testing>
    - npm run lint, npm run typecheck, npm test (Vitest domain/demo/time/runtime/SDK tests plus
      node:test for graph replay, seed-invite and the worker), npm run build. Last recorded run on this branch
      (plans/STATUS.md): 114 Vitest, 26 node tests; 67 pgTAP assertions after step 1a.
    - SQL/RLS tests: supabase/tests/archive_rls.sql, community_rls.sql (file name kept), invitations_rls.sql
      (supabase test db).
    - Local-stack browser e2e passed manually on 2026-11-14 (docs/VALIDATION.md).
    - Gaps: no automated browser tests, no hosted multi-user smoke test, no accessibility review, the projector
      has not run against a real Neo4j.
  </testing>

  <deployment>
    Order for the first hosted release: create the Supabase project → db push the migrations → seed the first
    invite with `just seed-invite confirm=hosted` → configure Auth site and redirect URLs and email → import the repo into
    Vercel with env vars → add the domain in Vercel and its DNS records in Cloudflare → optional Neo4j Aura
    with manual sync → optional worker. Smoke-test with two real accounts before inviting the group.
    Details: docs/RUNBOOK.md.
  </deployment>

  <operations>
    - Monitor Vercel errors, Supabase Auth failures, pending outbox count and age, sync lock age.
    - The graph lock never expires; after a crash, confirm no runner is active, then clear it manually.
    - A full graph rebuild requires keeping the outbox history (see RUNBOOK).
    - Set up backups before storing real memories.
  </operations>

  <requirements name="Next steps plan">
    Phases run in order; each one ends with its acceptance check passing. Keep each phase to small
    pull requests. Status values: [in progress], [next], [later], [done].
    Current position (2026-11-14): phases 0 and 1 (steps 1a-1e) are done on feature/base-tbellini-site (commit
    b0de4bd). Phase 2 is [next] and was replanned on 2026-11-14: its code parts (2.1 seed script, 2.6 health
    ping) are done; the provisioning runs (2.2, 2.3, 2.4, 2.5, 2.5b, 2.9 first run, 2.8) need the user's approval.
    Step log: plans/STATUS.md.

    <detail name="Phase 0 — Fix invite-only signup [done: commit 5810760, merged 2026-10-17]">
      Delivered: repaired the invitations migration (an in-place edit to an existing migration; acceptable only because
      the broken version could never have applied, so no hosted database ran it); RLS with no client access; member-only create_invitation RPC; atomic single-use consumption with
      used_by; random demo codes; invite-code validation and a clear error on signup; supabase/tests/invitations_rls.sql;
      ci.yml now targets site-code; RUNBOOK first-account seeding; docs updated for invites.
      Verified on the branch: lint and typecheck clean, 45 Vitest plus 18 node tests, build, db reset with all four
      migrations, 54 pgTAP assertions, and a manual local Auth check (unknown, reused, and out-of-range cases rejected).
      Not verified: hosted Supabase Auth. Details: docs/VALIDATION.md, "Invitations hardening — October 17, 2026".
    </detail>
