    </detail>
    <detail name="Phase 6 — Memories and connections (M3) [later]">
      Work: Memory entity and composer that links to any node; memories on every node page; a members-only graph
      read API for "connected to" and "why connected"; enable a scheduled graph sync.
      Acceptance: write a memory linking an event, an artist, and two members; it appears on all four pages
      with the connection explained.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Phase 7 — Timeline and search (M4) [later]">
      Work: unified timeline with filters (year, member, artist, place, type); search across all entities;
      replace whole-snapshot loading with paged, filtered queries.
      Acceptance: find any event, artist, place, or memory by text; timeline filters combine.
    </detail>
    <detail name="Phase 8 — Extended archive (M5) [later]">
      Collections and labels, astrology, historical import of past shows, approximate dates, cleanup tools, export.
    </detail>
  </requirements>

  <decisions>
    - Archive-first; the earlier social-network framing is superseded. No public feed.
    - One group, one shared space. There is no community or membership entity, and no per-user private
      records; a profile means "can log in" (2026-10-17, supersedes the 2026-10-06 community and private-people model).
    - Invite-only registration (commit 28a2536).
    - Only the creator edits or deletes a record (2026-10-17). RSVPs are self-only; tags never imply RSVPs.
    - People without accounts are shared Person entities, linked to a Member when they sign up (2026-10-17).
    - Claiming: an invite can name a Person, or the new member picks "this is me" and the Person's creator confirms.
      Every Member automatically gets a Person (answered and implemented 2026-11-14; plans/STATUS.md lists the
      user's review of these as pending).
    - Privacy = signed-in only: a profile is the only gate to read anything; there is no per-user private data.
    - Invites expire in 1–30 days, default 7, as built.
    - Any member can create invites; there is no admin role (2026-10-17).
    - Spotify is the music data provider (2026-10-17).
    - Supabase is the source of truth; Neo4j is a derived, rebuildable projection.
    - Vercel hosts the app; Cloudflare handles DNS, plus media/edge features when needed.
    - Migrations are forward-only and immutable once applied.
  </decisions>

  <constraints>
    - Never expose the service-role key, Neo4j credentials, or Spotify secrets to the browser or NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars.
    - Never let graph availability block a write; the browser never queries Neo4j directly.
    - Nothing is readable without signing in.
    - Finish phases in order; keep Linear changes inside tbellini-m.
  </constraints>

  <focus name="Known issues (2026-11-14)">
    - Nothing is provisioned (Supabase, Vercel, Cloudflare, Neo4j); the first-invite gap is solved in code by
      `just seed-invite` but not yet run against a hosted project.
    - The Neo4j projector has not run against a real Neo4j; old graphs need a wipe and full replay.
    - Adapter-to-Postgres behaviour is covered only by pgTAP (the Supabase contract test uses an in-memory fake).
    - The generated invite code is shown only behind a Copy button, not as text.
    - Remaining architecture debt is listed in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, section 9.
  </focus>

  <questions>
    - Invites are single-use and expire in 1–30 days (default 7), as built. Is that right?
    - Do other members' "I went" records about someone need that person's confirmation?
    - Model festivals as a Series plus yearly editions with day/stage child events? (This is the proposed default.)
    - What does astrology mean for this group (member birth charts, the sky at events, both)?
    - Resolved 2026-11-14: region us-east-2; no production domain yet (SITE_URL is the Vercel production URL).
  </questions>
</project>
